---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const traverseCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'dist', label: 'leg length', min: 20, max: 200, step: 5, value: 100, unit: 'm' },
    { name: 'angerr', label: 'angle error per leg', min: 2, max: 30, step: 1, value: 10, unit: '″' },
    { name: 'legs', label: 'legs in traverse', min: 2, max: 12, step: 1, value: 6, unit: '' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'angular misclosure (angerr × √legs)', expr: 'angerr*sqrt(legs)', unit: '″' },
    { label: 'position error at closure', expr: 'dist*angerr*sqrt(legs)/206265*legs', unit: 'm' },
  ],
};

const errorCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · No measurement is exact', note: 'Every observation carries random error. Surveying is the science of knowing how much, and of which direction it averages out.' },
    { math: '2 · Systematic vs random', note: 'Systematic errors follow a rule (a tape too long, a collimation tilt) and can be corrected; random errors only average and must be managed statistically.' },
    { math: '3 · Blunders are a third category', note: 'Mistargeting, transcription slips — prevented by procedure (double-centring, closed traverses), not by statistics.' },
    { math: '4 · Errors propagate', note: 'Independent random errors add in quadrature: σ_result² = Σσᵢ². Angles over a long traverse wander with the square root of leg count, not the count.' },
    { math: '5 · Closures prove the work', note: 'A traverse that returns to its start reveals its own error — the closure is the surveyor\'s receipt, and it must balance before anyone adjusts.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/surveying/foundational/surveying-mathematics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Surveying is applied geometry with legal consequences — and its mathematics is dominated by
    one discipline: the management of error. Every angle and distance is slightly wrong, so the
    surveyor's real craft is measuring how wrong the network is, making it average out, and
    proving the work with closures.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="errors: systematic, random, blunders — and propagation" widget="equationStep" config={errorCfg} />

  <h2>Error grows with the square root, not the count</h2>
  <p>
    The most useful single fact in surveying: independent random errors add in quadrature. A
    traverse of six legs, each with a 10-second angle error, closes with about 24 seconds of
    angular misclosure — not 60. Longer traverses wander more slowly than intuition fears, and
    (per leg length) shorter legs make the position error worse. Drag the sliders and check both.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="traverse misclosure vs leg count, length and precision" widget="sliderLab" config={traverseCfg} />

  <h2>Closures: the surveyor's receipt</h2>
  <p>
    Because error is inevitable, honest surveys are designed to check themselves: traverses close
    on their starting point, level loops return to their start height, and the residuals reveal
    the accumulated error before adjustment distributes it. A survey that never closes is a
    survey you cannot defend — which matters twice over when the corners define who owns what.
  </p>

  <h2>Geodesy changes the geometry</h2>
  <p>
    Over distances, the Earth stops being flat: lines converge, ellipsoid heights differ from
    sea-level heights, and grid bearings drift from true. The FS exam's mathematics section
    includes geodesy for this reason — the modern surveyor computes on a curved reference
    ellipsoid, with the flat-Earth version as a local approximation, not a truth.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FS exam specifications; Ghilani &amp; Wolf, <em>Elementary Surveying</em>; Buckner, <em>Surveying Computations</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
